Stores all system users who can log into Odoo, defining their access rights, preferences, and personal information.
Key Functional Use Cases:

Login credentials and multi-company access
Assignment of user groups / access rights (Sales, Accounting, Inventory, etc.)
Employee linking (HR integration)
Personal preferences (language, timezone, signature, dashboard)
Approval workflows and notifications
Salesperson / Purchaser assignment on documents
POS cashier, Warehouse operator, Accountant roles

Technical DNA:

Model Type: models.Model (persistent, core system model)
Key Fields:
login (char, required, unique), password
partner_id → res.partner (linked contact, required)
company_id (main company), company_ids (Many2many — allowed companies)
groups_id → Many2many to res.groups
active (boolean), tz, lang
signature, image_1920
notification_type (email/inbox), odoobot_state
Computed: name (from partner), email

Critical Hooks: create, write (password hashing, group changes), _check_credentials, action_reset_password
Security: Extremely sensitive — record rules + group-based access control

RAG Routing Keywords:
user, res.users, system user, login user, Odoo user, internal user, portal user, employee user, salesperson, cashier, user groups, access rights, user profile
Related Models (most joined):

res.partner (linked contact)
res.groups (via groups_id)
res.company (company_id / company_ids)
hr.employee (linked employee)
mail.channel, mail.message (notifications)
Almost every transactional document (sale.order.user_id, purchase.order.user_id, etc.)

Ultra-Dense Vector Paragraph:
res.users is Odoo’s core user model storing login, password, linked partner_id, allowed companies, groups_id (access rights), language, timezone, and signature. It controls system authentication, multi-company access, permissions, notifications, and is referenced as user_id / salesperson on sales orders, purchases, tasks, documents, and throughout the entire Odoo platform.